===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|fr|iconoclaste}}, from {{der|en|gkm|εἰκονοκλάστης|lit=image breaker}}. {{surf|en|icono-|-clast}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/aɪˈkɒn.əˌklæst/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/aɪˈkɑn.əˌklæst/|a=US}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-iconoclast.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-iconoclast.ogg|a=AU}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|historical|Christianity}} One who destroys religious images or [[icons]], especially an opponent of the [[Orthodox Church]] in the 8th and 9th centuries, or a [[Puritan]] during the European [[Reformation]].
#: {{ant|en|iconodule}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|author=Eugene TeSelle|section=Iconoclast|title=World Book Encyclopedia, 2004 edition (CD)|passage=In the days of the early Christian church, people who opposed the veneration (reverence) of images were called '''iconoclasts'''.}}
# One who opposes [[orthodoxy]] and [[religion]]; one who adheres to the [[doctrine]] of [[iconoclasm]].
#* '''2010 The Handbook of Texas Online''', ''William Cowper Brann'', Texas State Historical Association, Austin [http://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/online/articles/fbr23]:
#*: In February 1895 he [William Cowper Brann, 1855-1898 ] revived publication of the '''Iconoclast'''. This time it was successful and eventually attained a circulation of 100,000. Brann took obvious relish in directing his stinging attacks upon institutions and persons he considered to be hypocritical or overly sanctimonious.
# {{lb|en|by extension}} One who attacks cherished beliefs; a [[maverick]].
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.theatlantic.com/business/archive/2007/11/ugly-questions/2308/|title=Ugly questions|work=The Atlantic|date=2007-11-29|author=Megan McArdle|passage=After all, the fact that any discussion of the possibility is greeted with hysterical revulsion guarantees that only two types of people will take the "pro" side in public: fearless '''iconoclasts''' who do not care what anyone thinks of them; and racists.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.theatlantic.com/entertainment/archive/2016/04/prince-dead-at-57-lyrics-mortality/479370/|title=Prince the Immortal|work=The Atlantic|date=2016-04-21|author=Spencer Kornhaber|passage=Coming so soon after the death of David Bowie, it’s a moment to consider how deeply important '''iconoclasts''' are to the evolution of a culture. Do they change the world by trying to change it, or do they change the world through the radical act of simply being themselves?}}
#* {{quote-journal|en-US|author=George Johnson|title=Freeman Dyson, Math Genius Turned Technological Visionary, Dies at 96|work=The New York Times|date=2020-02-28|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/02/28/science/freeman-dyson-dead.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=Relishing the role of '''iconoclast''', he confounded the scientific establishment by dismissing the consensus about the perils of man-made climate change as “tribal group-thinking.”}}

